# Data Retention and Deletion Standard — R-2 and R-3 retention periods

> Module Retention

This module answers: for how long is a record retained, absent a Legal Hold?
It encodes the R-3.1 period table and its two limb-specific exceptions. The
Legal Hold override in R-4 lives in `Legal_hold`, which consumes
`retention_end_date` from here — R-4.1 is expressed as "notwithstanding R-3",
so it must sit below the whole of this module's output rather than inside it.

**CE-0020.** R-6.1's backup bound used to be computed here, as
`retention_end_date + 90 day`. That was the wrong trigger. R-6.1 runs its
90-day cycle from deletion **from production systems**, and this scope cannot
see that event: R-4.1 and R-4.3 can defer production deletion for years past
the R-3 expiry, and R-1.2 lets a business unit bring it forward. R-6.1 is now
encoded once, in `Legal_hold`, immediately below the clauses that fix the
production-deletion date — see the R-6 section of that module for the full
reasoning. This scope answers only the R-3 question it can answer, and offers
no backup date that a reader could mistake for one.

R-1.2 (PROSE) makes every period here a **maximum**: a computed
`retention_end_date` is a ceiling, never an instruction to keep. R-2.4 and
R-7.2 (PROSE) qualify it further. Those caveats are attached by the vector
engine, not restated here.

## Prologue — declarations

| DATA-RET R-2.1 (006-data-retention-standard.md:26)
|
| "Data Class" means the classification assigned to a record under the
| Company's information classification policy, being one of: Employee
| Record, Customer Contract, Financial Record, Marketing Contact, Security
| Log, or Candidate Record.

| DATA-RET R-2.2 (006-data-retention-standard.md:31)
|
| "Retention Trigger" means the event from which the retention period is
| measured, as specified in R-3.

R-2.1 contributes the closed six-valued enumeration on which the whole R-3.1
table is keyed; the classification itself is assigned under the information
classification policy and so arrives as an input. R-2.2 contributes
`retention_trigger_date`, the date of the event R-3.1 measures each period
from — which event that is differs per limb and is stated in R-3.1 itself.

`statutory_retention_applies` and `statutory_retention_period` are the two
**judgement inputs** of R-3.3: whether pensions or payroll legislation
requires a longer period, and how long, is a legal determination this
Standard does not contain.

`most_recent_engagement_date` is the second of the two dates R-3.1(d) takes
the later of; it is `Absent` for a contact who has never engaged.
`talent_pool_consent_date` is `Absent` where the candidate has given no
talent-pool consent, and `consent_renewal_dates` lists each recorded renewal
of that consent.

```catala-metadata
declaration enumeration DataClass:
  -- EmployeeRecord
  -- CustomerContract
  -- FinancialRecord
  -- MarketingContact
  -- SecurityLog
  -- CandidateRecord

declaration scope RetentionEnd:
  input data_class content DataClass
  input retention_trigger_date content date
  input most_recent_engagement_date content optional of date
  input talent_pool_consent_date content optional of date
  input consent_renewal_dates content list of date
  input statutory_retention_applies content boolean
  input statutory_retention_period content duration
  output retention_end_date content date
```

| NO-CLAUSE: the rounding mode is a compiler directive, not a rule. Catala
| date addition raises on an ambiguous result, and this scope adds month and
| year durations to arbitrary trigger dates (6 years from 29 February, 13
| months from 31 January), so a mode must be declared. `date round down` is
| chosen because R-1.2 states that where this Standard specifies a maximum
| period business units are expected to delete earlier: the period is a
| ceiling, so resolving an ambiguous month-end to the previous existing day
| never authorises retention for a day the Standard does not allow.
| Rounding up would extend every such period by a day.
|
| That justification is about the Standard's own periods, and R-3.3's period
| is not one of them: it is imposed by pensions or payroll legislation, and a
| statutory retention period is a floor rather than a maximum. Rounding it
| down would have the Company destroy a record a day before the legislation
| allows — a breach, where rounding an R-1.2 maximum down is at worst early
| deletion the Standard positively encourages. So R-3.3 rounds the other way,
| by calling `Date.add_round_up` explicitly rather than relying on the scope
| mode, exactly as `NdaSurvival` rounds its periods of protection up
| throughout. The scope mode stays `date round down` because every other
| period in this scope is an R-1.2 maximum, and limb (a)'s own six years
| keep rounding down even where R-3.3 displaces the result.
|
| CE-0029. That round-up is available only where R-3.3 actually governs, and
| R-3.3 governs only where the statutory period is LONGER than limb (a)'s six
| years. Applied to a statutory period that is not longer it does not protect
| a statutory floor at all — it lengthens the Standard's own maximum by a day,
| which is the one thing `date round down` was chosen to prevent. The
| engagement of R-3.3 is therefore tested in its condition, below, and not
| left to a maximum taken over the two dates.

```catala
scope RetentionEnd:
  date round down
```

## R-3 Retention periods

| DATA-RET R-3.1 (006-data-retention-standard.md:45)
|
| Records are retained for the following periods measured from the
| applicable Retention Trigger:
|
|   (a) Employee Record: 6 years from the end of employment;
|   (b) Customer Contract: 7 years from the expiry or termination of the
|       contract;
|   (c) Financial Record: 7 years from the end of the financial year to
|       which it relates;
|   (d) Marketing Contact: 24 months from the later of the date of
|       collection and the date of the most recent engagement by the
|       contact;
|   (e) Security Log: 13 months from the date of the logged event;
|   (f) Candidate Record: 12 months from the date on which the recruitment
|       process concluded.

Each limb gets its own label, because R-3.2 and R-3.3 are exceptions to
*named limbs* — (f) and (a) — and not to the table as a whole.

Limb (d) is the only one whose trigger is a maximum over two dates: the 24
months run from whichever of `retention_trigger_date` (collection) and
`most_recent_engagement_date` is later. Running them from the collection date
alone would under-retain every contact who has since engaged.

Durations keep the clause's own units — `6 year`, `24 month`, `13 month` —
because Catala days and months are incomparable.

```catala
scope RetentionEnd:
  label r3_1_a definition retention_end_date
    under condition data_class with pattern EmployeeRecord
    consequence equals retention_trigger_date + 6 year

  label r3_1_b definition retention_end_date
    under condition data_class with pattern CustomerContract
    consequence equals retention_trigger_date + 7 year

  label r3_1_c definition retention_end_date
    under condition data_class with pattern FinancialRecord
    consequence equals retention_trigger_date + 7 year

  label r3_1_d definition retention_end_date
    under condition data_class with pattern MarketingContact
    consequence equals
      (match most_recent_engagement_date with pattern
       -- Absent: retention_trigger_date
       -- Present content engaged:
            Date.max of retention_trigger_date, engaged)
      + 24 month

  label r3_1_e definition retention_end_date
    under condition data_class with pattern SecurityLog
    consequence equals retention_trigger_date + 13 month

  label r3_1_f definition retention_end_date
    under condition data_class with pattern CandidateRecord
    consequence equals retention_trigger_date + 12 month
```

| DATA-RET R-3.2 (006-data-retention-standard.md:58)
|
| By way of exception to R-3.1(f), where the candidate has given consent to
| be retained on a talent pool, the Candidate Record is retained for 24
| months from the date of consent, and the period restarts on each renewal
| of consent.

An exception to `r3_1_f` alone, and the condition repeats that limb's own
condition — `data_class with pattern CandidateRecord`. A Catala exception
does not inherit its parent's condition, so omitting it would apply the
talent-pool period to an Employee Record or a Security Log whenever a consent
date happened to be on file.

"The period restarts on each renewal of consent" means the 24 months run from
the most recent of the original consent and every recorded renewal, so the
consent date and the renewal list are combined with a maximum. Taking the
maximum over the renewals alone would regress to an earlier date if the
register held only renewals predating the consent.

```catala
scope RetentionEnd:
  label r3_2 exception r3_1_f definition retention_end_date
    under condition
      (data_class with pattern CandidateRecord)
      and (talent_pool_consent_date with pattern Present)
    consequence equals
      (match talent_pool_consent_date with pattern
       -- Absent: retention_trigger_date
       -- Present content consented:
            Date.max of
              consented,
              (maximum of consent_renewal_dates
               or if list empty then consented))
      + 24 month
```

| DATA-RET R-3.3 (006-data-retention-standard.md:62)
|
| By way of exception to R-3.1(a), records required to be retained for a
| longer period under pensions or payroll legislation are retained for the
| period so required.

**HYBRID.** The consequence is arithmetic, but whether pensions or payroll
legislation requires a longer period — and how long — is a legal
determination this Standard does not contain, so both are judgement inputs.

An exception to `r3_1_a` alone, and again the condition repeats that limb's
condition `data_class with pattern EmployeeRecord`: without it, a standing
determination that payroll legislation applies would lengthen the period for
Customer Contracts and Security Logs too.

The statutory period runs from the same Retention Trigger as limb (a), the
end of employment. The clause bites only where the statutory period is the
*longer*, which means a supplied period shorter than six years cannot cut
limb (a) down.

It is also the one period here that is a **floor rather than a maximum**, so
it is the one that rounds *up*: resolving ten years from 29 February back to
28 February would destroy the record a day before the legislation permits,
which is the one direction rounding must not err in. Hence the explicit
`Date.add_round_up` against the scope's own mode, argued in full in the
rounding note above. Limb (a)'s six years keep rounding down inside their
R-1.2 maximum, so one record can see both directions — correctly, because
the two periods are different kinds of thing.

**CE-0029.** "A longer period" is a comparative, and until now the module did
not test it: the condition asked only whether a statutory period applied, and
the comparison was left to a `Date.max` over limb (a)'s date and the
rounded-up statutory date. On a statutory pensions period of exactly six years
— equal, not longer — that put the round-up to work where R-3.3 does not
engage at all, and an Employee Record whose employment ended on 29 February
2020 came back as retainable until 1 March 2026 where R-3.1(a) read with R-1.2
allows 28 February 2026. The module's two routes to the same legal quantity
disagreed by a day: the same trigger with `statutory_retention_applies` false
gave 28 February, and the same six years expressed as 72 months gave 1 March.
A day of retention the Standard does not authorise is a real defect, because
R-1.2 makes its periods maxima and the whole point of holding a record is that
it is held.

So the comparative is now in the condition. The statutory period is compared
with limb (a)'s six years as a period — by resolving both from the same
Retention Trigger, which is the only way the Standard offers to compare them,
Catala's months and days being incomparable as durations — and R-3.3 engages
only where the statutory period reaches beyond limb (a)'s own date. That
comparison resolves the statutory period *downwards*: the question it asks is
whether the period exceeds six years as a length of time, and a period that
exceeds it only once rounded up (six years from 29 February) does not. Where
R-3.3 does engage, the floor reasoning above is untouched and the period is
resolved upwards, so a genuine ten-year statutory period still runs to 1 March
2030. The `Date.max` is kept as the invariant it always was — R-3.3 cannot
shorten limb (a) — but on the narrowed condition it can no longer be the thing
that decides the answer.

| DATA-RET R-3.3 (006-data-retention-standard.md:62)
|
| By way of exception to R-3.1(a), records required to be retained for a
| longer period under pensions or payroll legislation are retained for the
| period so required.

The period so required cannot run backwards, and R-3.3 is what fixes that
bound. What the clause receives is a period for which records "are required to
be retained ... under pensions or payroll legislation": a length of time for
which a record must be kept, and the shortest such requirement is to keep it
for no time at all. A negative period is not a requirement to retain anything,
and the clause's own comparative — "a **longer** period" — presupposes a period
that can be compared with limb (a)'s six years as a duration of retention. The
bound is therefore the clause's and not a programming convenience, and the
scope declines rather than taking the later of two dates one of which precedes
its own trigger.

Two points of technique, both about staying inside what the clause allows.

The guard is asserted only where the R-3.3 judgement is that a statutory
period applies. `statutory_retention_applies` and `statutory_retention_period`
are one judgement in two inputs, and where the first is false the second is
not a period the Standard is relying on at all — asserting against it then
would refuse a question R-3.3 answers perfectly well, which is the over-tight
guard's own kind of defect.

And the bound is expressed by adding the period to the trigger date rather
than by comparing the period with `0 day`. A statutory period may well be
stated in mixed units — six years and six months, say — and Catala's days and
months are incomparable, so `statutory_retention_period >= 0 day` raises
"ambiguous comparison between durations in different units" on exactly those
lawful inputs. That is verified, not assumed: the duration comparison holds for
a pure-month or pure-day period and raises for a mixed one. Date addition is
total under the declared rounding mode, so the date form guards every period
the clause can supply and refuses only the ones it cannot.

```catala
scope RetentionEnd:
  assertion
    if statutory_retention_applies
    then (Date.add_round_up of
            retention_trigger_date, statutory_retention_period)
         >= retention_trigger_date
    else true

  label r3_3 exception r3_1_a definition retention_end_date
    under condition
      (data_class with pattern EmployeeRecord)
      and statutory_retention_applies
      or (Date.add_round_down of
             retention_trigger_date, statutory_retention_period)
          > retention_trigger_date + 6 year
    consequence equals
      Date.max of
        (retention_trigger_date + 6 year),
        (Date.add_round_up of
           retention_trigger_date, statutory_retention_period)
```
